incompressible-fluid-phase-space-identit.interpretation:
In a Hamiltonian system, the time evolution of a system's state is described by a vector field in phase space, where phase space is spanned by generalized coordinates 
(
$q_i$
) and their conjugate momenta 
(
$p_i$
). The velocity of a point in phase space is given by 
(
$\dot{q}_i$
) and 
(
$\dot{p}_i$
), which are determined by Hamilton's equations: 
(
$\dot{q}$_i = \frac${\partial H}{\partial p_i}$
) and 
(
$\dot{p}$_i = -\frac${\partial H}{\partial q_i}$
), where 
(
$H$
) is the Hamiltonian of the system. The divergence of this velocity field, 
(
$\nabla \cdot \mathbf{v}$
), measures the rate at which infinitesimally small volumes in phase space expand or contract. The formula shows that for any Hamiltonian system, this divergence is identically zero. This means that while individual states may evolve, the total volume occupied by any ensemble of states remains constant. Mathematically, this is shown by substituting Hamilton's equations into the divergence expression: 
(
$\frac{\partial \dot{q}_i}{\partial q_i} + \frac{\partial \dot{p}_i}{\partial p_i} = \frac{\partial}{\partial q_i} \left( \frac{\partial H}{\partial p_i} \right) + \frac{\partial}{\partial p_i} \left( -\frac{\partial H}{\partial q_i} \right) = 0$
) due to the symmetry of second partial derivatives of the Hamiltonian.
